{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested for exact 82f067e6704d79371b0cc7d17d548631081718b4, tree 2b236dc985ad4497abe77287a87004f42310fbca. Primary 8c27b21d was read first and in full (444 lines). This supersedes no verdict on draft 1: that request was released by d33cf60e before I filed one.\n\n1. Fidelity: the note follows the requested builder-room direction, keeps executable effects in definitions, and makes checks and scenario expectations separate. The shortened draft aligns section 9's summary with sequencing draft 2. Its concrete forms and slice obligations still leave the implementation choices below unresolved.\n2. Correctness: changes requested, F1–F5 below.\n3. Evidence: complete design review, not runtime approval. All 2,189 regular Git leaves/modes/OIDs compared with parent Main32b32fc2: exactly one new note, all 2,188 prior leaves unchanged. Four relative links exist; whitespace clean; shared checkout clean. Runtime and tests unchanged. Base tree b7362d1e11a68a4f7585a877113f6a196d81de2a; candidate tree 2b236dc985ad4497abe77287a87004f42310fbca. Per docs/testing.md's documents-only rule, no repeated gate. Scratch /private/tmp/checker-builder-room-draft2-ap0n9bqq/validation.json. Source contracts, current package types, Grow's actual sends/handlers and plan033 were checked; no private M2a checkout or dependencies read.\n\nF1 (P1), lines166–178, 381–382: the refusal alternative has no deadline or reason. The string late:\"refuse\" has neither within nor reason, and there is no separate lag bound in ReceiveType. Thus two identical handlers at source time0/commit time5 cannot express refusal after10 versus after1. The branch alternative has both fields, but selecting the string loses them. Put the bound and reason outside the branch/refuse choice, or put them in each alternative, and specify when both branch and ordinary guards/attention are considered. State the spacing/time clock-behind behavior and branch precedence sufficiently to replay one answer. Require fresh, boundary and late controls for both alternatives. Separating applied-branch from refusal does answer the conceptual F7 issue; the current wire form does not finish it. The cited LF deadline is also a specified interval plus a handler, not a handler alone (https://www.lf-lang.org/docs/writing-reactors/deadlines/).\n\nF2 (P1), lines75–103, 123–148: define how connection declarations bind actual scope instances and slots before claiming port compatibility by construction. The tuple (from definition digest, slot name, to definition digest) is ambiguous when two item types use the same slot name, and a definition digest does not name one of several created instances. The groups list likewise has only definition digests, whereas GroupRead.member names an enrolled instance. Current scope-valued fields/slots constrain kind, not the target's pinned definition: an installation could declare A.slot -\u003e B, populate it with another same-kind scope C, and pass this static graph while the actual message is refused unknown-message. State a bounded endpoint/creation-path and member-name binding, how native creation/slot assignment enforces it, and what can be proved for outside endpoints whose declarations are absent. Alternatively qualify this as checking the proposed graph only, with actual binding an explicit later obligation. The manifest can stay declarative; the lowering or native check must make its claimed connection true.\n\nF3 (P2), lines212–221: the states form has no defined subject. Neither DeclaredDefinition nor the package adds a designated mode item. Current definitions have several item types; acts use on (sometimes null), handlers use opens/also, and timed rules identify their type with on. A bare states:[\"open\"] cannot identify the scope's mode item or say what happens for a comment with no primary item. Declare that binding and its validation, or define states as sugar over a named existing subject and item type. Give a witness with two item types sharing a state name, rather than leaving P4 to invent the semantics.\n\nF4 (P2), lines129–144, 379–388: stage the validator and its witnesses by the forms that exist. P1 depends only on M2a but owes every section3 check, including group reads/order from S3, late from S1/P2, reachability from P4, and patches/parameters from P5. P4/P5 themselves depend on P1. Moreover warning unreachable-state, warning sequenced-for-nothing and the projected-bound report are not named refusals, so P1's requirement that each check have a refusal pair contradicts the table. Name P1's actual implemented subset, explicit unsupported forms and bounds, and each later check's owner/dependency; distinguish refusal, warning and report witnesses. Reconcile S1/P2 ownership of late so the filed slices have one concrete completion contract.\n\nF5 (P1), lines223–233: remove or prove the unconditional \"with staleness at or above lifetime it never waits.\" Sequencing draft2 resolves an allocation before the member writes. A member can stop after resolution and before commit; that allocation is final resolved and never expires, but at(X) must answer behind until the missing entry exists. An older cut can include this allocation even with s \u003e= lifetime. The group clock and expiry of open allocations do not make the missing resolved entry available. This is the same recovery/progress issue in the companion sequencing review, and the promise must be corrected here too. Preserve missing-dependency/behind instead of treating it as empty.\n\nOpen policy choices Q1–Q6 are not themselves blocking. No new judgment was found in the manifest's written guards/effects because it has none; F2 concerns whether its wiring and instance names have an enforceable meaning. No source or provider mutation was performed.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:8c27b21d5a83bee0f778590c473d49102961e492","binding":"assigned","head":"82f067e6704d79371b0cc7d17d548631081718b4","